# SOP: Creating Knowledge Docs

> **How to use this template:** Replace `[knowledge base]` with your team's knowledge tool (e.g., Guru, Notion, Confluence, Google Drive, Dropbox, SharePoint) and `[messaging tool]` with your team's chat tool (e.g., Slack, Teams). Everything else applies as written.

## Purpose & Objective

This SOP is a guide for the team on how to create knowledge docs in the `[knowledge base]`. The output of the process it describes is a published, peer-reviewed knowledge doc.

## Definition of Success

A good knowledge doc is easy to use, easy to understand, and easy to apply.

Success is when team members are using this document — following it to quickly and easily create knowledge docs.

## Example of Successful Output

A well-created knowledge doc works two ways:

- An AI can be pointed at it as training and context.
- A human can read it and understand how the process works.

## Prerequisites & Inputs

- Access to the `[knowledge base]`. Anyone on the team can submit a draft — no special permissions are required.
- A `[messaging tool]`, for the drafter to notify reviewers that a draft is ready, and for the reviewer to notify the drafter when review is complete.
- Knowledge of which folder or space the doc belongs in (typically your own team's folder).

## Procedure

### Step 1: Check the knowledge base for similar or duplicate documents

Before starting work on a new knowledge doc, search the `[knowledge base]` for similar or duplicate documents. The purpose is to avoid recreating work that already exists.

**Expected output:** A clear picture of whether related documentation already exists.

### Step 2: Surface any duplicates to the drafter

If the check turns up a similar existing doc, the drafter should be informed of:

- What that document is
- A summary of what it consists of
- A link to that document (or documents)

**Expected output:** The drafter has enough information to make an informed decision in Step 3.

### Step 3: Decide whether to update the existing doc or create a new one

The person drafting the new knowledge doc has the freedom and authority to decide whether to update the existing document or create something new.

- If updating a document you authored: proceed.
- If updating a document someone else authored: inform the author at minimum. Ideally, ask the author's permission before updating.

**Expected output:** A decision to either update an existing doc or create a new one.

### Step 4: Draft directly in the knowledge base

Draft the document directly in the `[knowledge base]` rather than drafting it elsewhere. Do not draft in a separate word processor or notes app and copy and paste it over.

**Tools:** `[knowledge base]`

**Expected output:** A draft in progress in the `[knowledge base]`.

### Step 5: Add the front matter

Every knowledge doc begins with front matter.

**Format:** A bullet list, with each front matter field name in bold followed by a colon, a space, and then the value. Each field goes on its own bullet.

**Example:** **author**: Pete Larkin

The seven fields:

- **type**: required. Declares what the concept is (e.g., `table`, `metric`, `runbook`, `api`)
- **title**: a clean, human-friendly display name for the concept
- **description**: a concise summary of the file's context
- **resource**: a URL or unique reference pointing to the actual technical asset (e.g., a database string or documentation link)
- **tags**: an array of key terms used for filtering and index categorization
- **timestamp**: the date and time when the record was captured or updated
- **author**: the person who created the doc

**Expected output:** Complete front matter at the top of the doc.

### Step 6: Write the knowledge doc body

The knowledge doc goes underneath the front matter.

There are no structural expectations for the body — no required sections. However, these content rules apply:

- Do not link out to PDFs, presentations, or other assets unless there is a unique edge case. Anything we want in the knowledge base is stored directly in the `[knowledge base]` rather than linked to.
  - Example: if the doc discusses a brand guide, the brand guide itself lives in the `[knowledge base]` — you do not simply link to a file in an external storage tool.
- Reference other internal content by its native ID (doc ID, card ID, page ID) rather than by a fragile link or title.
- Linking to file assets is acceptable — for example, images, videos, or fonts.
- Typically, we want everything to live inside the `[knowledge base]`.

**Expected output:** A complete draft body.

### Step 7: Add reviewers and notify them

Once the draft is written, hand it off for review:

- Add the team members who may need to review it to the draft.
- Send those reviewers a message in the `[messaging tool]` telling them the draft is ready for their review, and include a link to the doc that needs to be reviewed.

**Tools:** `[knowledge base]`, `[messaging tool]`

**Expected output:** The relevant reviewers have access to the draft and know it is waiting on them.

### Step 8: Peer review

Every final output must have peer review. The reviewer can be:

- Someone you work with laterally, or
- Someone you work with above you, such as a manager.

Reviewers comment and suggest edits directly in the `[knowledge base]`.

**Expected output:** Comments and suggested edits resolved, and the reviewer satisfied.

### Step 9: Reviewer notifies the drafter

After the person conducting the peer review has finished, they send a message in the `[messaging tool]` to the drafter / creator of the knowledge doc to inform them the review is complete.

**Tools:** `[messaging tool]`

**Expected output:** The drafter knows review is done.

### Step 10: Publish

Once peer review is finished and everything looks good, the person who drafted it publishes it for use.

**Expected output:** A live knowledge doc.

### Step 11: Place the doc in the right folder

Each team has their own folder that they work in, but teams are not always constrained to that folder.

- Typically, most people build knowledge docs to live within their own team's folder.
- If a doc is shared across the entire team, it may be moved into another team's folder.

**Expected output:** The doc lives in an appropriate folder.

### Step 12: Ongoing feedback and improvement

Once knowledge docs are created and available, anyone on the team who has access can comment and suggest edits. This is how input and feedback from other team members is taken in to improve the docs over time.

**Expected output:** A living document that improves with team input.

## Edge Cases & Exceptions

- **Linking out.** Linking out to PDFs, presentations, or other assets is permitted only in a unique edge case. The default is to store the content in the `[knowledge base]`.
- **Cross-team docs.** A knowledge doc that serves the entire team may be moved out of the author's team folder into another team's folder.
- **Updating someone else's document.** The drafter still has authority to decide, but must inform the original author at minimum, and ideally ask permission.

## Tool-Specific Notes

Some tools handle parts of this process differently. Fill in whatever applies to yours:

| Process element | What to define for your tool |
| --- | --- |
| Draft state | Does the tool have a native draft/unpublished state, or do you use a "Draft — do not use" folder? |
| Front matter | Rich-text bullet list, YAML frontmatter, or database properties/fields? |
| Reviewer assignment | Native reviewer/verification feature, @mention, share permission, or assigned task? |
| Commenting | In-tool comments and suggestions, or a separate review thread? |
| Publishing | A publish action, moving out of a drafts folder, or changing a status field? |
| Internal references | Doc ID, card ID, page ID, or file URL? |
| Ownership / verification | Does the tool track an owner and prompt periodic re-verification? |
